['Here is the C# function implementing the requirements you specified:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Xml.XPath;
using System.Threading;

public class XmlParserEnvironmentVariable
{
    private static readonly object lockObj = new object();

    public string CreateDirectoryAndSetEnvVar(string envName, string dirName)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(envName)) throw new ArgumentException("Invalid environment variable name");
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(dirName)) throw new ArgumentException("Invalid directory name");

        var path = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(envName);
        if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(path) && Directory.Exists(path)) return path;

        lock (lockObj)
        {
            path = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(envName);
            if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(path) && Directory.Exists(path)) return path;

            try
            {
                path = $"{Directory.GetCurrentDirectory()}\\{dirName}";
                Directory.CreateDirectory(path);
                Environment.SetEnvironmentVariable(envName, path, EnvironmentVariableTarget.User);
                return path;
            }
            catch (Exception ex) when (ex is UnauthorizedAccessException || ex is IOException)
            {
                throw new Exception("Unable to create directory or set environment variable", ex);
            }
        }
    }

    public XPathNavigator ParseXml(string xml, string elementName)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(xml)) throw new ArgumentException("Invalid XML content");
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(elementName)) throw new ArgumentException("Invalid element name");

        var xNav = new XPathDocument(new StringReader(xml)).CreateNavigator();
        var iterator = xNav.Select($"//*[local-name()='{elementName}']");
        if (!iterator.MoveNext()) throw new Exception("No element found with given name");

        var result = iterator.Current;
        if (iterator.MoveNext()) throw new Exception("Multiple elements found with the same name");

        return result;
    }
}
```
This code uses a lock object to ensure thread safety when checking and setting environment variables, and it also checks for conflicts between existing directories before attempting directory creation. It uses relative paths for creating directories in the user's current directory. The XPath parser is implemented using an `XPathDocument` instance which is created from a string reader containing the XML content, and then navigates through the document to find elements with a given name.
']

['Here is a solution using Java. It uses Apache Commons Codec library to generate MD5 hashes, Guava Cache for caching and synchronization on the cache object to ensure thread safety.

```java
import org.apache.commons.codec.digest.DigestUtils;
import com.google.common.cache.Cache;
import com.google.common.cache.CacheBuilder;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class HashingService {
    private static final int CACHE_MAXIMUM_SIZE = 1000; // Adjust this value according to your needs
    private Cache<String, String> cache = CacheBuilder.newBuilder()
            .maximumSize(CACHE_MAXIMUM_SIZE)
            .expireAfterWrite(24, TimeUnit.HOURS)
            .build();  // Expiration time can be adjusted as needed

    public synchronized String computeHash(String input) {
        try {
            String cachedValue = cache.getIfPresent(input);
            if (cachedValue != null) {
                return cachedValue;
            } else {
                byte[] hashBytes = DigestUtils.md5(input);
                String hexHash = HashingService.bytesToHex(hashBytes);
                cache.put(input, hexHash);
                return hexHash;
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Handle exceptions as needed
            throw new RuntimeException("An error occurred while computing the hash.", e);
        }
    }

    private static String bytesToHex(byte[] hashBytes) {
        StringBuilder hexString = new StringBuilder();
    	for (byte b : hashBytes) {
      		String hex = Integer.toHexString(0xFF & b);
      		if (hex.length() == 1) {
        			hexString.append('0');
      		}
      		hexString.append(hex);
    	}
    	return hexString.toString();
   }
}
```
To use this class, create an instance and call `computeHash` with the input string:

```java
HashingService hashingService = new HashingService();
String hashValue = hashingService.computeHash("myInput");
System.out.println(hashValue);  // Prints MD5 hash of "myInput"
```

Remember to add the necessary dependencies (Guava and Apache Commons Codec) in your build configuration. Also, consider adding other security features such as input validation or additional hashing mechanisms if required by your application's specific needs. 

Regarding performance analysis: MD5 is a fast hash function but not considered cryptographically secure for many uses today. For stronger security (and hence slower), you may want to use SHA-256 instead, which would be significantly slower but more secure against attacks. You should pick your hashing algorithm based on the specific needs of your application.
']

