['```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.security.*;
import java.util.*;

public class DuplicateFileFinder {
    public static int findDuplicates(List<Path> paths) throws IOException, NoSuchAlgorithmException {
        Set<String> uniqueFiles = new HashSet<>();

        for (Path path : paths) {
            long startTime = System.nanoTime();

            MessageDigest md5Digest = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
            try(InputStream is = Files.newInputStream(path)){
                byte[] buffer = new byte[8192];
                int bytesRead;
                while((bytesRead = is.read(buffer)) != -1){
                    md5Digest.update(buffer, 0, bytesRead);
                }
            }

            String digest = Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(md5Digest.digest());
            uniqueFiles.add(digest);

            long endTime = System.nanoTime();
            long durationInNanos = (endTime - startTime); 
            System.out.println("Processing " + path + " took: " + durationInNanos + " nano seconds");
        }

        return paths.size() - uniqueFiles.size();
    }
}
```
This Java function uses the MD5 hash to compare files' contents, which is more efficient than comparing every byte of each file. We use a HashSet instead of a List or another collection type because it allows us to efficiently check for duplicates and add new hashes without having to iterate over all elements in the collection.

We also calculate and print how long it took to process each file path, giving insights on performance. This gives you an idea of what might cause performance issues if there are too many files. 

Make sure that your function is thread-safe as necessary for multithreaded environments. Consider using concurrent collections or synchronization primitives as needed.

This code assumes the existence and accessibility of all input file paths and does not handle any exceptions related to missing, inaccessible or otherwise unreadable files. If you want your function to be robust against such issues, you need to add error handling code accordingly.
