['Here is a simple example of how you could implement this in Rust using the `reqwest` library to create an HTTP client:

```rust
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
use std::time::Duration;
use reqwest::ClientBuilder;
use hyper_tls::HttpsConnector;
use url::Url;
use reqwest::Error as ReqwestError;
use failure::Fail;

#[derive(Debug, Fail)]
enum HttpError {
    #[fail(display = "HTTP request failed")]
    RequestFailed,
}

pub struct HttpClientManager {
    client: Arc<Mutex<ClientBuilder>>,
}

impl HttpClientManager {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        let https_connector = HttpsConnector::with_native_roots().unwrap();

        let client = ClientBuilder::new()
            .timeout(Duration::from_secs(30))
            .redirect(reqwest::redirect::Policy::limited(10))
            .connector(https_connector)
            .build().unwrap();

        HttpClientManager { client: Arc::new(Mutex::new(client)) }
    }

    pub async fn request(&self, url: Url, headers: Option<Vec<(&str, &str)>>) -> Result<String, ReqwestError> {
        let mut client = self.client.lock().unwrap();

        match headers {
            Some(headers) => {
                for (key, value) in headers {
                    client = &mut **client.default_header(key, value);
                }
            },
            None => {}
        }

        let res = client.get(url).send().await?;

        if !res.status().is_success() {
            return Err(ReqwestError::Status(res));
        }

        Ok(res.text().await?)
    }
}
```
This is a very basic example, and would need to be expanded significantly to implement features such as token-based authentication, dynamic configuration of the auth service endpoint/credentials, handling network failures or timeouts during token acquisition, graceful fallback mechanisms in case of network partitions, easy integration with future external services, and so on. 

Please note that error handling in this code is pretty rudimentary as well - you'll probably want to do something more sophisticated (perhaps using a logger) rather than simply panicking or returning raw errors. Similarly, the `ClientBuilder` is locked for use and modified within each method call, which might not be efficient if you are making many concurrent requests. 

Also, please note that in a real-world application, you would want to handle these cases more gracefully (e.g., by retrying the request, falling back to an alternative service, etc.) rather than panicking and exiting the entire program.
