char* appendSlashIfNotExceedSizeLimit(const char* str, size_t sizeLimit){
    if (str == NULL || sizeLimit == 0){
        return NULL; // returning NULL for null input or zero limit
    }

    size_t len = strlen(str);
    if (len > sizeLimit){
        return NULL; // returning NULL if string length exceeds the limit
    }

    char* resultStr = malloc((len + 2) * sizeof(char)); // allocating memory for result string with an additional place for null character and slash
    if (resultStr == NULL){
        return NULL; // return NULL if allocation fails
    }

    strcpy(resultStr, str); // copy the original string to new location
    resultStr[len] = '/'; // appending slash
    resultStr[len + 1] = '\0'; // adding null character at end

    return resultStr;
}
```
This function allocates just enough memory for the resulting string. It uses `strcpy` to copy the original string, and then manually adds a slash and null character to it. If any allocation fails or if the input string length exceeds the limit, it returns NULL without leaking memory.

2) JavaScript recursive function:
```javascript
function traverseDOM(node, selectorTestFunc){
    let result = []; // initializing empty array for results

    (function recurse(node){
        if (!node || typeof node !== 'object'){ // base case when we've reached a non-object node
            return; 
        }

        for (let i = 0; i < node.childNodes.length; i++){ // recursively calling function on each child
            recurse(node.childNodes[i]); 
        }

        if (selectorTestFunc && selectorTestFunc(node)){ // checking whether the current element matches the provided selector
            result.push(node); // adding matching elements to results array
        }
    })(node);

    return result; // returning final result
}
```
This function uses a closure inside to avoid polluting global scope with additional variables, and recursively traverses the DOM tree using depth-first search. It keeps track of matching elements in an array, which is returned at the end. The `selectorTestFunc` parameter can be any function that takes a node as input and returns whether it matches some selector (like checking if its class or ID matches).
"]

