['Your requirements are quite complex to be implemented in a single function due to numerous factors such as managing errors, handling signals, executing multiple processes, parsing configuration files, integrating logging functionality, thread safety etc. Therefore, this task will need to be broken down into smaller tasks and implemented piece by piece using various functions and data structures. Here's a high-level structure of how you could potentially implement this:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct {
    char* env_vars; // Array of environment variables, each in the format "key=value"
    int argc;       // Number of arguments
    char** argv;    // Arguments
} CommandConfig;

void handleSignal(int signal) {
    // Ignore SIGINT and SIGTERM signals
    if (signal == SIGINT || signal == SIGTERM) {
        return;
    }
}

void executeCommand(char* command, CommandConfig config) {
    pid_t pid = fork();

    if (pid == 0) { // Child process
        for (int i = 0; i < config.argc; i++) {
            putenv(config.env_vars[i]); // Set environment variables
        }

        execvp(command, config.argv); 
    } else if (pid < 0) { // Error occurred
        printf("Fork failed\n");
        return;
    } else { // Parent process
        int status;
        wait(&status);

        if (WIFEXITED(status)) {
            printf("%s exited with code %d\n", command, WEXITSTATUS(status));
        } else {
            printf("Command failed\n");
        }
    }
}

void executeMultipleCommands(char* commands) {
    char *command = strtok(commands, ";");

    while (command != NULL) {
        // Load config for command and handle errors
        CommandConfig config;

        if (!loadConfig(&config)) {
            printf("Failed to load config\n");
            return;
        }

        executeCommand(command, config);
        command = strtok(NULL, ";"); // Get next command
    }
}

int main() {
    signal(SIGINT, handleSignal);
    signal(SIGTERM, handleSignal);

    char* commands; // Load commands from config file or other source
    executeMultipleCommands(commands);

    return 0;
}
```

This is a basic structure and it lacks many features which you requested such as: loading configurations, logging functionality and thread safety. For production quality code, we would also need to consider error handling in all areas of this example (file operations, memory management etc). Also, the design needs to be revised and modified according to your specific requirements and constraints.
']

