import java.io.*;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class Deserializer {
    private static ConcurrentHashMap<String, Object> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public static <T> T deserialize(byte[] byteArray) throws RuntimeException {
        try (ByteArrayInputStream bis = new ByteArrayInputStream(byteArray);
             ObjectInput in = new ObjectInputStream(bis)) {
            String cacheKey = new String(byteArray);  // Assuming a unique key can be created from the byte array
            if (cache.containsKey(cacheKey)) {
                return (T) cache.get(cacheKey);
            } else {
                T obj = (T) in.readObject();
                cache.putIfAbsent(cacheKey, obj);  // ConcurrentHashMap's putIfAbsent avoids redundant computation for same byte array
                return obj;
            }
        } catch (ClassNotFoundException | IOException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to deserialize the object", e);
        }
    }
}
```
This code implements a caching mechanism using ConcurrentHashMap which is thread-safe and efficient for concurrent access. The cache key in this case is generated from the byte array, assuming that each unique byte array corresponds to a unique object. This might not be practical if the byte arrays are very large or come from external data sources where uniqueness cannot be guaranteed without additional processing.
