Java Code (with caching):
```java
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class HashCache {
    private static final int MAX_CACHE_SIZE = 10000;  // Limit the size of cache to prevent memory issues.
    private static volatile HashMap<String, byte[]> cachedHashes = new HashMap<>();

    public synchronized static String getMD5Hash(String input) {
        byte[] hashBytes = cachedHashes.get(input);

        if (hashBytes == null) {
            MessageDigest md;
            try {
                md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
            } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
                // Handle exception gracefully.
                return null;
            }

            hashBytes = md.digest(input.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
            cachedHashes.put(input, Arrays.copyOf(hashBytes, hashBytes.length)); 

            // If cache size limit is reached, remove the oldest entry to keep memory usage under control.
            if (cachedHashes.size() > MAX_CACHE_SIZE) {
                cachedHashes.remove(cachedHashes.keySet().iterator().next()); 
            }
        }

        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for(byte b : hashBytes){
           sb.append(String.format("%02x", b));
        }

        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```
The above Java method uses the built-in `MessageDigest` class from java.security package to compute MD5 hash of a string input. The computed hashes are stored in a cache (HashMap) for quick access and reuse if needed again. If the cache size reaches limit, it removes the oldest entry (based on insertion order) before adding new entries.

The Java method is thread-safe as its execution is synchronized so that only one thread can execute it at a time. This ensures data integrity when multiple threads call this function concurrently.
']

