['The code you're asking for involves implementing a large-scale system from scratch which can be complex and time consuming. It requires understanding of C++ programming, Boost library, networking protocols (like HTTP GET/POST), use of external libraries like JSON Schema validation libraries, integration with logging systems, caching mechanisms, thread safety, memory management optimization, handling network failures etc.

Here's a simplified example that shows how you might start to implement this system in C++ using the Boost library and a basic GET/POST request:

```cpp
#include <boost/asio.hpp>
#include <iostream>
#include <istream>

using boost::asio::ip::tcp;

void http_get(std::string url) {
    try {
        boost::asio::io_service io_service;

        // Get the HTTP server's address and port number.
        std::string server = "http://" + url;
        tcp::resolver resolver(io_service);
        tcp::resolver::query query(server, "80");
        tcp::resolver::iterator endpoint_iterator = resolver.resolve(query);

        // Try each endpoint until we successfully establish a connection.
        tcp::socket socket(io_service);
        boost::asio::connect(socket, endpoint_iterator);

        // Form the request. We specify the "Connection: close" header so that the
        // server will close the socket after transmitting the response. This will
        // allow us to treat all data up until the EOF as the content.
        boost::asio::streambuf request;
        std::ostream request_stream(&request);
        request_stream << "GET / HTTP/1.0\r\n";
        request_stream << "Host: " << url << "\r\n";
        request_stream << "Accept: */*\r\n";
        request_stream << "Connection: close\r\n\r\n";

        // Send the request.
        boost::asio::write(socket, request);

        // Read the response status line. The response streambuf will automatically
        // grow to accommodate the entire line. The growth may be limited by passing
        // a maximum size to the streambuf constructor.
        boost::asio::streambuf response;
        boost::system::error_code error;
        while (boost::asio::read(socket, response, 
            boost::asio::transfer_at_least(1), error)) {}

        // Write the response to standard out.
        std::cout << &response;
    } catch (std::exception& e) {
        std::cerr << "Exception: " << e.what() << "\n";
    }
}

int main() {
    http_get("www.boost.org");

    return 0;
}
```
Please note that this code is very basic and does not include all the features you specified in your prompt, such as POST requests or integration with an external logging system. Also it doesn't handle rate limiting, caching, thread safety optimization or network timeouts/retries. You would have to implement these separately based on the specific requirements of your project. 

