['Here is the C code block that meets the described requirements. This solution uses POSIX threads for thread safety, and dynamically allocates memory as needed to handle large amounts of data efficiently.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>

#define MAX_STR_LEN 100 // Adjust as needed

typedef struct {
    int value;
    char str[MAX_STR_LEN];
} data;

static pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

data* init_data(int num_records, int seed) {
    if (num_records <= 0 || seed < 0) {
        printf("Error: Invalid input parameters.\n");
        exit(1);
    }

    data *records = malloc(sizeof(data) * num_records);
    if (records == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed.\n");
        exit(2);
    }

    srand(seed + time(NULL)); // Initialize seed value for random number generation

    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex); // Ensure thread safety
    for (int i = 0; i < num_records; i++) {
        records[i].value = rand();
        sprintf(records[i].str, "Record %d", i+1);  // Assign string value to each record
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);

    return records;
}
```
This code initializes a dynamic array of `data` structures using the provided seed value for random number generation. The mutex lock is used to prevent other threads from accessing the shared resource while it's being modified, thus ensuring thread safety. The function also includes error checking and handling for invalid input parameters and memory allocation failures.
']

