['Here is a skeleton for the function in Rust language as per your requirement and some of the trade-offs you mentioned. However, this implementation needs more details such as how external data sources will be used to determine the compiler flags for different file types, handle integration issues with other tools or services interacting with the function etc.

```rust
use std::process::{Command, Stdio};
use std::fs;
use std::path::PathBuf;
use std::time::Instant;
use std::env;
use libc::size_t; // for resident set size measurement

fn compile_rust_files(inputs: Vec<String>, output_dir: String, additional_flags: Vec<String>) -> Result<i32, &'static str> {
    let start = Instant::now();

    // Create the log file in the specified directory
    fs::create_dir_all(&output_dir).expect("Failed to create output directory");
    let mut logfile = fs::File::create(PathBuf::from(&*format![ "{}/log", output_dir])).unwrap(); 

    // Handle multiple input files with different compilation flags
    for (i, file) in inputs.iter().enumerate() {
        // Use external data sources to determine compiler flags for each file type
        let mut compiler = Command::new("rustc");
        compiler.arg(file);

        if i < additional_flags.len() {
            compiler.arg(&additional_flags[i]);
        }

        // Redirect output to the log file
        let status = compiler.stdout(Stdio::piped())
                              .stderr(Stdio::piped())
                              .spawn().unwrap()
                              .wait_with_output();

        writeln(&mut logfile, &*format![ "Compiled {}", file]).expect("Log failed"); 
        writeln(&mut logfile, &String::from_utf8(status.stderr).unwrap()).expect("Log failed");
    }

    let elapsed = start.elapsed().as_secs(); // Calculate elapsed time

    // Measure resident set size (RSS) before and after compilation
    let rss: size_t;
    unsafe {
        libc::process_vm_readv(libc::getpid() as _, ptr::null_mut(), 0, &rss, 1, 0);
    }

    // Log the elapsed time and resident set size to standard error
    eprintlnln![ "Elapsed time: {} seconds", elapsed];
    eprintlnln![ "Resident Set Size (RSS): {} bytes", rss as usize];

    // Return exit code of subprocess
    Ok(status.unwrap().code().unwrap())
}
```
This function should be called with command line arguments using a `structopt` or similar crate in the main function:

```rust
fn main() {
    let opt = compile_args::from_args(); // assuming you have defined your own struct 'compile_args'
    if let Err(err) = compile_rust_files(opt.inputs, opt.output_dir, opt.additional_flags) {
        println![ "Error: {}", err];
        std::process::exit(1);
    }
}
```
This is just a basic implementation to give you an idea of how this function might be structured and should be adjusted based on your specific requirements and constraints. It's also worth noting that error handling, integration issues, external data sources, etc, are complex topics in their own right and would require further investigation or possibly the use of more advanced libraries/tools for a full implementation.
']

