```java
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;

/**
 * This method calculates MD5 hash of a given string parameter.
 * 
 * @param input The input string for which the MD5 hash is to be calculated.
 * @return A hexadecimal representation of the MD5 hash of the input string.
 */
public static String calculateMD5(String input) {
    try {
        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
        byte[] messageDigest = md.digest(input.getBytes());

        // Convert byte array into signum representation
        BigInteger no = new BigInteger(1, messageDigest);

        // Convert message digest into hex value
        String hashtext = no.toString(16);
        while (hashtext.length() < 32) {
            hashtext = "0" + hashtext;
        }

        return hashtext;
    } 
    catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
        throw new RuntimeException(e);
    }
}
```
This method calculates MD5 hash of a given input string. If an exception occurs during the calculation process, it is caught and re-thrown as a runtime exception to maintain the robustness of the system. 

Please note that you need to import necessary classes for this Java code to work correctly: java.security.MessageDigest, java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException, java.math.BigInteger. Also, please remember that MD5 is a weak hash function and should not be used for secure purposes (like storing passwords). Consider using stronger algorithms like SHA-256 or even better, bcrypt which has been specifically designed to slow down attacks as attackers try to guess passwords.
