["Here is a basic implementation in C which uses a linked list and mutexes for thread safety. 

```C
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct LTEPHYControlPDU {
    int UE_ID;
    int PHICH_resources;
    char *PHICH_indication_tag;
    bool PHICH_detected_flag;
    struct LTEPHYControlPDU* next;
} LTEPHYControlPDU;

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER; 
LTEPHYControlPDU *head = NULL; // Initialise list head to NULL.

// Add a new node with UE_ID and PHICH resources into the linked list.
int addNode(int ueId, int phichResources) {
    LTEPHYControlPDU* newNode = (LTEPHYControlPDU*) malloc(sizeof(LTEPHYControlPDU)); // Allocate memory for new node.
    if (!newNode) return -1;  // Memory allocation failed, return error code.

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);  // Lock mutex before accessing the list.

    newNode->UE_ID = ueId;
    newNode->PHICH_resources = phichResources;
    srand(time(0));
    newNode->PHICH_detected_flag = rand() % 2; // Randomly set PHICH_detected_flag.
    newNode->next = head;
    head = newNode;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);  // Unlock mutex after accessing the list.

    return 0;
}
```
This code handles memory management for large numbers of structures by dynamically allocating memory for each node using malloc, which avoids buffer overflow issues and ensures efficient memory usage. However, this design trade-off comes with a trade-off of potentially slower performance due to dynamic memory allocation. Furthermore, the mutex locks are used to ensure thread safety by only allowing one thread to access the list at a time. 

This code snippet does not include error handling for UE_ID registration and PHICH resource detection as it wasn't specified in the task. It is assumed that these checks would be done before adding a new node to the list, but could easily be incorporated into this function if necessary.
